Bash Script
As part of the project, I created a Bash script called:
website-check.sh
The purpose of this script is to check whether my cloud-hosted website is online. The script uses curl to request the website and return the HTTP status code. If the website returns status code 200, the script prints that the website is online. If another status code is returned, the script prints a warning message.
The script is stored in this repository under:
scripts/website-check.sh
Script code:
#!/bin/bash

# ICT171 Assignment 3 - Website Availability Checker
# This script checks whether my cloud-hosted website is online.

# Store the website URL in a variable
URL="https://arinmehla.info"

# Use curl to request the website and return only the HTTP status code
STATUS=$(curl -o /dev/null -s -w "%{http_code}" "$URL")

# If the HTTP status code is 200, the website is responding successfully
if [ "$STATUS" -eq 200 ]; then
    echo "Website is online. Status code: $STATUS"
else
    echo "Website may be down. Status code: $STATUS"
fi
To make the script executable, I used:
chmod +x website-check.sh
To run the script, I used:
./website-check.sh
The output was:
Website is online. Status code: 200
This script is useful because it provides a quick command-line method to check whether the Azure-hosted website is responding correctly.
Testing and Validation
The following tests were completed to confirm that the server was working correctly.
Nginx Service Test
sudo systemctl status nginx
Result:
Active: active (running)
Website File Test
ls -l /var/www/html
This confirmed that the website files were stored in the correct Nginx web directory.
HTTPS Website Test
curl -I https://arinmehla.info
Result:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
This confirmed that the website was online and accessible through HTTPS.
HTTP Redirect Test
curl -I http://arinmehla.info
Result:
HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently
Location: https://arinmehla.info/
This confirmed that HTTP traffic redirected to HTTPS.
Nginx Configuration Test
sudo nginx -t
This command was used to confirm that the Nginx configuration had no syntax errors.
Script Test
./website-check.sh
Result:
Website is online. Status code: 200
This confirmed that the Bash script worked correctly.
Problems Encountered and Fixes
Problem 1: Azure Subscription Stopped
At one point, my Azure for Students subscription ran out of available credit, which caused the website to stop running. I fixed this by reactivating the Azure subscription and starting the virtual machine again.
Problem 2: Ping Packet Loss
When I used the ping command, the domain resolved to the correct IP address but showed 100% packet loss.
The output showed:
arinmehla.info (20.92.249.85)
This confirmed that DNS was working, but the server was not replying to ICMP ping packets. I learned that ping can be blocked by cloud firewall or network rules. I confirmed the website was working by using:
curl -I https://arinmehla.info
This returned:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Problem 3: HTTPS Localhost Error
When I tested:
curl https://localhost
I received an SSL certificate error. This happened because the SSL certificate was issued for arinmehla.info, not localhost.
I fixed this by testing the correct domain name instead:
curl -I https://arinmehla.info
Problem 4: Bash Script Syntax Error
While creating the Bash script, I received this error:
syntax error: unexpected end of file
This happened because the Bash if statement was not closed correctly. I fixed the issue by adding:
fi
at the end of the if statement.
What I Learned
Through this project, I learned how to create and manage a cloud server using Infrastructure as a Service. I gained experience with Azure virtual machines, SSH, Linux commands, Nginx configuration, DNS records, HTTPS testing and Bash scripting.
I also learned that server testing should use more than one method. For example, ping may fail because of blocked ICMP traffic, even when the website is still working. In this project, curl was a more useful test because it checked the actual HTTP and HTTPS response from the website.
Conclusion
This project successfully demonstrates the creation and configuration of a cloud-hosted web server using Microsoft Azure, Ubuntu Linux and Nginx. The server hosts a custom Classic Cars Showcase website and is accessible through a custom domain name with HTTPS enabled.
The project also includes a Bash script to check website availability and GitHub documentation that explains the setup process clearly.
